<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Theoretical analysis of diversity-inducement methods</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let each candidate response <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> have a latent quality <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. The
model also assigns an internal signal, such that the confidence score, <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, which
is used for candidate selection. In practice, since the true quality <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown
at test time, the practical selector chooses the candidate</p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}=\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}S(y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}=\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}S(y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We can approximate the joint distribution of <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q,S)</annotation></semantics></math> as a bivariate
distribution. This distribution has means <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, variances
<math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{Q}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{Q}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{S}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{S}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and correlation <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\mathrm{Corr}(Q,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Corr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\mathrm{Corr}(Q,S)</annotation></semantics></math>. The expected quality of the selected candidate can then be expressed as:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Q(y^{*})]\approx\mu_{Q}+\rho\sigma_{Q}k_{N}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Q(y^{*})]\approx\mu_{Q}+\rho\sigma_{Q}k_{N},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{N}=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}Nz\,\varphi(z)\,\Phi(z)^{N-1}\,dz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{N}=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}Nz\,\varphi(z)\,\Phi(z)^{N-1}\,dz</annotation></semantics></math> is the
expected maximum of <math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> standard normal variables. Here, <math id="A1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z)</annotation></semantics></math> is the
standard normal probability density function, and <math id="A1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(z)</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard normal
cumulative distribution function. Notably, <math id="A1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> grows slowly as the candidate
pool size <math id="A1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Temperature sampling generates candidates with high variance in quality,
<math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, these samples are often drawn from low-likelihood regions,
where the model’s internal confidence <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(y)</annotation></semantics></math> is poorly aligned with the true quality
<math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. As a result, the correlation <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="A1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is small, which
leads to weak scaling as more candidates are added.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, input perturbations combined with greedy decoding produce candidates
with lower variance but higher mean quality <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. More importantly, the correlation
<math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is stronger, because these responses remain on the likelihood manifold
where the model was trained to assign high confidence. This difference arises from
the training objective of language models: next-token prediction under maximum
likelihood estimation. During training, the model is optimized for greedy
decoding, and temperature sampling is not simulated (e.g., there is no Gumbel-softmax trick
in training), making temperature sampling less natural for the model.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, language models are often miscalibrated, especially after post-training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
This miscalibration further reduces the correlation <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> for candidates
from temperature sampling.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Under confidence-based selection, the product <math id="A1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\sigma_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\sigma_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is provably larger
for greedy decoding with input perturbations than for temperature sampling. This
establishes greedy decoding with augmented inputs as a superior mechanism for
generating diverse candidates in test-time scaling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Theoretical analysis of token-level aggregation vs. answer-level aggregation</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider generating a response of length <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. Let <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the probability
of the base model generating the correct token at step <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> given the correct prefix,
with <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;p_{\min}\leq p_{t}\leq p_{\max}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;p_{\min}\leq p_{t}\leq p_{\max}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Token-level selection.</span> At each step <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> candidate tokens
are generated. A selector with accuracy <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (probability of selecting
the correct token if available) yields correctness probability <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}=s_{t}\left[1-(1-p_{t})^{N}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}=s_{t}\left[1-(1-p_{t})^{N}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the overall correctness probability is</p>
<table id="A2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{token}}=\prod_{t=1}^{T}q_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>token</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{token}}=\prod_{t=1}^{T}q_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answer-level selection.</span> <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> independent responses are generated. A selector
with accuracy <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> (probability of selecting the fully correct response if available)
yields a correctness probability given by</p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{answer}}=s\left[1-\left(1-\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{t}\right)^{N}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{answer}}=s\left[1-\left(1-\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{t}\right)^{N}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem.</span> Assume there exists <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}\geq(1+\delta)p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}\geq(1+\delta)p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for sufficiently large <math id="A2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>,
token-level selection achieves a higher expected correctness probability, <math id="A2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{token}}&gt;P_{\text{answer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>token</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{token}}&gt;P_{\text{answer}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> From the assumption <math id="A2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}\geq(1+\delta)p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}\geq(1+\delta)p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{token}}\geq(1+\delta)^{T}\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{t}=(1+\delta)^{T}P_{\text{correct}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>token</mtext></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>correct</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{token}}\geq(1+\delta)^{T}\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{t}=(1+\delta)^{T}P_{\text{correct}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{correct}}=\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>correct</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{correct}}=\prod_{t=1}^{T}p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. For answer-level selection:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{answer}}\leq s\cdot N\cdot P_{\text{correct}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>correct</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{answer}}\leq s\cdot N\cdot P_{\text{correct}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">since <math id="A2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-(1-x)^{N}\leq Nx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-(1-x)^{N}\leq Nx</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Comparing the two:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{P_{\text{token}}}{P_{\text{answer}}}\geq\frac{(1+\delta)^{T}P_{\text{correct}}}{sNP_{\text{correct}}}=\frac{(1+\delta)^{T}}{sN}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>token</mtext></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>correct</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>correct</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{P_{\text{token}}}{P_{\text{answer}}}\geq\frac{(1+\delta)^{T}P_{\text{correct}}}{sNP_{\text{correct}}}=\frac{(1+\delta)^{T}}{sN}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p5.4" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\delta)^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\delta)^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> grows exponentially with <math id="A2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="A2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sN</annotation></semantics></math>
is constant. Therefore, for sufficiently large <math id="A2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{(1+\delta)^{T}}{sN}&gt;1\implies P_{\text{token}}&gt;P_{\text{answer}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>token</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{(1+\delta)^{T}}{sN}&gt;1\implies P_{\text{token}}&gt;P_{\text{answer}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feasibility of <math id="A2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}\geq(1+\delta)p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}\geq(1+\delta)p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> The condition holds if:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}\geq(1+\delta)\frac{p_{t}}{1-(1-p_{t})^{N}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}\geq(1+\delta)\frac{p_{t}}{1-(1-p_{t})^{N}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-(1-p_{t})^{N}&gt;p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-(1-p_{t})^{N}&gt;p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the right-hand
side <math id="A2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, there exists <math id="A2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the inequality. For typical
<math id="A2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}\in(0.5,0.99)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.99</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}\in(0.5,0.99)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, reasonable <math id="A2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="A2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.7-0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.7</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.7-0.95</annotation></semantics></math>) suffice.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conclusion.</span> Token-level selection achieves superior performance because
it corrects errors immediately at each generation step, preventing error propagation
through the sequence. The per-token improvement factor <math id="A2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> compounds
multiplicatively across steps. In contrast, answer-level selection suffers from
exponential decay in correctness probability (<math id="A2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∏</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) and provides only
constant-factor improvement (<math id="A2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sN</annotation></semantics></math>) through response selection.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">This exponential scaling with sequence length means that token-level aggregation
provides a rapidly growing advantage as responses become longer, making it especially
effective for reasoning tasks such as chain-of-thought and thinking models. In these settings, each token represents a step in the reasoning process, so the
ability to correct errors at every step prevents error accumulation and leads to
much higher overall correctness compared to answer-level selection, whose benefits
do not scale with sequence length.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Also, the superiority of increased granularity aligns with empirical observations that
process reward models outperform outcome reward models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2023</a>)</cite>,
and reasoning step-wise approaches like step-level self-evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2023</a>)</cite>
and REBASE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> surpass answer-level methods. However, these reasoning
step-wise strategies remain limited to problems where reasoning steps can be clearly
defined and still fall short of token-level granularity. But, they exemplify a
general trend: increased granularity
yields better performance in test-time
scaling.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For autoregressive generation with imperfect selectors, token-level selection
achieves higher expected correctness than answer-level selection when the token selectors provide consistent multiplicative improvement over base probabilities
and the response length is sufficiently large. The critical advantage comes from
per-step error correction that mitigates compounding errors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Aggregation methods</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare different token-level aggregation methods for test-time augmentation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simple averaging</span> uniformly weights all augmented predictions by
computing the arithmetic mean of probability distributions across all augmented
inputs, as in Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.1 Test-Time Augmentation (TTAug) ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Efficient Test-Time Scalingfor Small Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>,
<math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}_{j}(v)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}p_{i,j}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}_{j}(v)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}p_{i,j}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Entropy-weighted averaging</span> assigns higher weights to more confident predictions
by computing the entropy <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H_{i,j}=-\sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}}p_{i,j}(v)\ln p_{i,j}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ln</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i,j}=-\sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}}p_{i,j}(v)\ln p_{i,j}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> for each augmented input <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> at step <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, deriving weights
<math id="A3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i,j}=e^{-H_{i,j}}/\sum_{k=1}^{N}e^{-H_{k,j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i,j}=e^{-H_{i,j}}/\sum_{k=1}^{N}e^{-H_{k,j}}</annotation></semantics></math> through softmax over
negative entropy, and aggregating as <math id="A3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}_{j}(v)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{i,j}p_{i,j}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}_{j}(v)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{i,j}p_{i,j}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chun et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Majority voting</span> aggregates predictions by selecting the token that receives
the most votes across augmented inputs. For each vocabulary token <math id="A3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> at step
<math id="A3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the vote count <math id="A3.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{j}(v)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}[\arg\max_{u\in\mathcal{V}}p_{i,j}(u)=v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>arg</mi><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{j}(v)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}[\arg\max_{u\in\mathcal{V}}p_{i,j}(u)=v]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator
function. The final token is selected as <math id="A3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}=\arg\max_{v\in\mathcal{V}}c_{j}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}=\arg\max_{v\in\mathcal{V}}c_{j}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, choosing the vocabulary token with the highest vote count across all
augmented predictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farina et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Most confident token</span> method selects the token with the highest
predicted probability across all augmented inputs, <math id="A3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}=\arg\max_{i,v}p_{i,j}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}=\arg\max_{i,v}p_{i,j}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the predicted probability offers a noisy proxy for confidence as shown by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2017)</a></cite>,
this approach effectively chooses the most confident token across all
augmentations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks &amp; Gimpel, 2017</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
